tunity to discard a card and leave the opponent in control.\n\nSo, I think playing the King is the best move to achieve my primary goal. It lets me shed a card, sets up my preferred play of the Full House, and it sets the stage for a strong finish if I retain the Rocket. It\'s my most aggressive, most logical move at this point. I play the King to control the flow and keep the Rocket in my hand.\n\n**Move:** ♦K\n\n**Reason:** Playing the King allows me to shed a single card, forcing the opponents to react and retaining the Rocket for maximum control.\n\n**Answer:** {"cards": ["♦K"]}\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current play to beat is a Single Q. I have a Single K, which is a perfect cover as it clears a loose card from my hand while forcing the opponents to use a top-tier card (Ace or 2) to beat it. My hand structure consists of a Full House (77788), two singles (10, K), and a Rocket (Double Joker).\nBy playing the King:\n1. I reduce my hand count.\n2. If the King clears, I can potentially run my Full House (77788) or play the Rocket to seize absolute control.\n3. I preserve the Rocket. This is crucial because there are four 6s unaccounted for, posing a Bomb threat. Breaking the Rocket (e.g., playing a single Joker) would leave me vulnerable to the bomb.\n4. If the opponent beats my King with an Ace or the last 2, I can decide to use the Rocket immediately to regain the lead and play my Full House.\n\nReason: The King is the most efficient play to advance the game. Passing allows the opponents to dictate the pace, and playing a Joker breaks the Rocket which is needed for insurance against potential bombs (specifically 6666) and to guarantee regaining the lead to play the Full House.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♦K"]}'}
